@prefix owl:   <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdfs:  <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:  <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix dct:   <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .
@prefix vcvf:  <http://www.ontologydesignpatterns.org/ont/values/valuecore_with_value_frames.owl#> .
@prefix vn:    <https://fandaws.com/ontology/valuenet/triggers-semantics#> .

# =====================================================================
# Semantics for vcvf:triggers
#
# vcvf:triggers is the most load-bearing relation in ValueNet: 57,578
# statements resolving to 147 distinct objects. ValueCore 0.4 declares it as an
# owl:ObjectProperty with rdfs:domain owl:Thing and rdfs:range vcvf:Value, and
# says nothing about what it means. This module supplies the definition and
# nothing else.
#
# It annotates a term it does not own. ValueCore is an upstream artefact at
# version 0.4 and is not edited here; the same rule that keeps bfo-core.ttl
# unpatched applies. An annotation asserted from a local module is additive
# and can be dropped by anyone loading ValueCore alone.
#
# Checked rather than assumed: under RDFS closure this module derives no
# ground triple about vcvf:triggers, about any ValueNet class, or about any
# trigger object. What it does derive is housekeeping about the annotation
# vocabulary itself -- that skos:definition is an rdf:Property, that the
# literals are rdfs:Resource. That is unavoidable for any annotation and
# touches no term in the corpus.
#
# Deliberately NOT here: rdfs:range. It is already asserted upstream, and it
# already entails -- under RDFS closure a trigger object becomes a vcvf:Value
# whether or not anyone declared it one. Nine trigger objects currently carry
# no type at all and are Values solely by that inference. Until each has been
# examined and declared, or corrected, adding to the range machinery would
# deepen a commitment nobody has made. See vcvf-triggers-review.md.
# =====================================================================

vn:  a owl:Ontology ;
     dct:license <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/> ;
     dct:title "Semantics for vcvf:triggers"@en ;
     rdfs:label "Semantics for vcvf:triggers"@en ;
     dct:description """Supplies the definition ValueCore 0.4 omits for
vcvf:triggers. Annotation only: no logical axiom is added, and under RDFS
closure it derives no ground triple about vcvf:triggers, any ValueNet class,
or any trigger object."""@en ;
     rdfs:comment "Annotates ValueCore without modifying it."@en .


vcvf:triggers
    skos:definition """A directional evocation relation.

The SUBJECT is a resource that can serve as a semantic or lexical trigger or
cue for the ValueNet concept denoted by the OBJECT. The subject evokes the
object; the object does not evoke the subject.

The relation asserts evocation and nothing more. In particular it does NOT
assert identity, equivalence, synonymy, subclass or superclass relationship,
or definitional authority. That <http://dbpedia.org/resource/An_Education>
triggers folk:Education says that the page can cue the value Education in
text; it does not say the value is the film, is defined by the film, or is a
kind of film."""@en ;

    skos:scopeNote """Subject position is deliberately heterogeneous. The
corpus uses lexical senses, encyclopedic entities and web resources as trigger
sources -- 12 distinct hosts including Wiktionary, DBpedia, BabelNet, YAGO,
UMBEL, PreMOn and WordNet -- and no narrow OWL domain is imposed on it. The
declared rdfs:domain owl:Thing reflects that and constrains nothing.

Object position is near-closed by contrast: 147 distinct objects across three
namespaces, all of them ValueNet value concepts."""@en ;

    skos:editorialNote """Direction is a total convention in the data and is
enforced by nothing. Across ThatsAllFolks, 18,668 trigger statements place the
external resource in subject position and zero place one in object position;
mf-triggers agrees. OWL cannot express the constraint, because the declared
range makes any object a Value by entailment rather than rejecting a wrong
one. It is a SHACL obligation."""@en ;

    rdfs:comment """Reading a trigger statement as an alignment is a known
misreading with consequences: it turns a defensible lexical-cue claim into an
indefensible identity claim. Run 1's MAPPING-002 drew a defect from exactly
that inversion and Run 3 archived the reading as settled."""@en .
